        "explanation": "Both images show the same integrated network, with only layout and styling differences.\n\n**Reference image**\n- Uses a spring layout, all on one plane.\n- It has 15 blue gene nodes: KRAS, NF1, BRCA1, PIK3CA, TP53, TERT, RB1, PTEN, CDKN2A, APC, EGFR, MYC, AKT1, COL18A1 and VEGFA.\n- It has 8 orange miRNA nodes: let-7a, miR-143, miR-155, miR-17, miR-200c, miR-21, miR-34a and miR-125b.\n- Hub genes are marked only by larger node size. Six nodes are clearly enlarged: KRAS, PIK3CA, TP53, EGFR, MYC and AKT1.\n\n**Candidate image**\n- Draws two separate layers: a PPI gene layer and a miRNA layer.\n- It has the same 15 genes and the same 8 miRNAs, with 91 edges in total.\n- Hub genes are filled red and listed in the title. They are the same six: KRAS, MYC, AKT1, TP53, EGFR and PIK3CA.\n- The legend gives the hub threshold (degree centrality \u2265 P90 = 0.636).\n\n**How the details compare**\n- **miRNA\u2013target edges:** The ones I checked look the same in both images. For example, let-7a links to KRAS and NF1, miR-143 to KRAS and PIK3CA, and miR-17 to VEGFA and a hub gene. Both also show the sparse genes COL18A1 and VEGFA linked mainly through miRNA or a few PPI edges.\n\n**Differences**\n- **Presentation only:** layout (spring vs. stacked layers), colours, node shapes and edge styles differ.\n- **Clearer in the candidate:** it marks the hubs with a distinct colour and adds a legend. The reference relies on node size only.\n- **Not checked:** I could not confirm every single edge, which is why confidence is slightly below maximum.\n\n**Conclusion:** The data, the two-layer structure and the hub set match, so the two images carry the same scientific message."
